  "query_preview": "..A weak shortwave ridge is building into Utah this\nevening ahead of a shortwave trough ejecting across the PacNW and\nIdaho, and as subtle low pressure over eastern Utah earlier today\nlifts northeast away from the area. Convection from earlier this \nafternoon has mostly waned, leaving patchy midlevel clouds. \nMeanwhile, the trough to the northwest will continue to eject into\nwestern Montana. This will push a cold front into northern Utah \ntonight, dropping temperatures a few degrees there through \nSaturday. The weather will be otherwise benign on Saturday, with \ndrier and more stable conditions under a somewhat breezy \nsouthwest flow.\n\nModels are still consistent in lifting a vort max originating \nnear Hurricane Odalys through Utah on Sunday. This will bring\nincreased moisture to the area, leading to isolated to widely\nscattered convection, mainly over the higher terrain. This will\nstart priming the airmass for the next storm system Monday and\nTuesday, which looks increasingly likely to tap tropical \nmoisture.\n\nCluster analysis of global ensembles are coming together on the\nidea of the splitty solution with a closed low tracking across \nArizona on Tuesday. Meanwhile, models are also favoring Hurricane\nPolo to cross into northwest Mexico Tuesday morning before its \nremnants move into New Mexico. The pattern favors tropical\nmoisture being ingested into the southwesterlies ahead of the\nclosed low, and model QPF has been increasing. However, the\nquestion is how much of this moisture makes it into Utah, given\nthe southerly track of the low. While the bulk of the moisture \nwill remain south and east of Utah, there are indications that\nportions of southern and southeast Utah will likely receive\nwidespread precipitation and potentially significant amounts. The\nlatest NBM has 25th percentile amounts Monday through Tuesday of a\nthird to a half an inch across southern through southeast Utah,\nwhile the 75th percentile has widespread 1.25-1.5 inches with\nlocally greater than 1.75 inches between St George and Kanab.\nLooking at it a different way, the southern third of Utah has \nroughly a 30-55% chance of exceeding 1 inch of rain between 12z \nMonday and 12z Wednesday. There is a Slight Risk for Excessive \nRainfall in that area Monday morning through Monday night. \nMeanwhile, northern Utah currently has less than a 30% chance of \nseeing even 0.5 inches over the same period (except over \nmountains). Will continue to monitor developments to see if any \nhydro highlights are needed for southern Utah in the near future.\n\nCooler temperatures are expected areawide Monday through Tuesday,\nwith afternoon maxes falling to 3-10 degrees below normal. \nTemperatures should rebound as the low exits the area Wednesday \nafternoon. However, there are uncertainties regarding the large- \nscale pattern for the latter half of the week, with ensembles \nsplit between another trough moving into the area and ridging or \nzonal flow patterns.",
